attrs.tooltip:
How the fog is distributed. Uniform fills the air evenly;
the height-based kinds pool it in the valleys and leave
the peaks clear, which is what morning mist actually does.

Convex ground (ridges, crests) resists the smoothing;
concave ground (gullies, hollows) takes it fully -
which is the shape hydraulic erosion carves.

How strongly slope drives incision. Above 1 the steep
reaches cut away fastest and the profile straightens out;
below 1 they persist.

How much of the flattened result replaces the
original. The full effect is strongly painterly;
part of it just calms a noisy surface.

How gradually the flank gives way to the flat top.
Low gives the sharp shoulder of a lava-capped mesa;
high gives a rounded summit.

How abruptly one step gives way to the next. Low
leaves rounded treads that still read as a slope;
high gives a flat tread and a near-vertical riser.

How large the patches of more and fewer stones are.
Low gives a couple of broad drifts across the map;
high breaks it into many small clusters.

How many times the pattern is added at a smaller size.
Each adds finer detail and costs about as much again;
once an octave is finer than a texel it buys nothing.

